<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="51126" href="schedule/2009/spring/LLS/396/51126.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2009" href="schedule/2009.xml">2009</calendarYear><term id="120091" href="schedule/2009/spring.xml">Spring 2009</term><subject id="LLS" href="schedule/2009/spring/LLS.xml">Latina/Latino Studies</subject><course id="396" href="schedule/2009/spring/LLS/396.xml">Adv Topics Latina/o Studies</course></parents><sectionNumber>LR </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Topic: "Reading Chicana/o Popular Culture." This course will examine how various forms of Chicana/o popular culture, including poetry, film, music and visual arts, act as discourse spaces of identity expression and theory production. We will employ literary and cultural theoretical analysis to explore how these spaces articulate the complex intersections of ethnicity, class, gender and sexuality found within Chicana/o identities.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2009-01-20Z</startDate><endDate>2009-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>09:00 AM</start><end>10:20 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>106B3</roomNumber><buildingName>Engineering Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Rodriguez" firstName="L">Rodriguez, L</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>